.Dd November 3, 2026
.Dt IMPSTAGE0 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm impstage0
.Nd the seed compiler for IMP72, written in C
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl S | Sir | c
.Op Fl o Ar output
.Op Fl s Ar letters
.Ar file.imp ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is the seed of the IMP72 compiler
.Xr imp 1 :
a C99 port of the kernel and of the code generator, which builds the stage
compilers.
It compiles programs in IMP72, the extensible systems language of Ned Irons as
implemented by Walter Bilofsky for the PDP-10 (DECUS 10-198a), to native code
for the machine it runs on.
IMP stands for (Ned) Irons Meta Processor: it is named for Ned Irons, who invented it, as LISP is named for its List Processor.
It has no
.Fl -target ,
.Fl I ,
.Fl i ,
.Fl -version
or
.Fl -help .
It reads the syntax of the language from
.Pa syntax.imc
and the C interface from
.Pa ffi.imc .
Its code generator writes the same LLVM IR as the code generator of
.Xr imp 1
for the same program.
.Pp
All the files named are compiled together as one program, and the output is
named after the first.
LLVM, in the process, makes the machine code.
Executables are linked with lld, in the process where it is installed as a
library and otherwise as
.Xr ld.lld 1
.Pq Xr ld64.lld 1
on macOS.
.Pp
IMP72 passes arguments by reference, and a word is 64 bits (32-bit hosts hold
it in a
.Vt long long ) .
A
.Sy SUBR
is therefore a C function of pointers to its arguments, returning a word, and a
C program can call it.
.Sy CCALL F(A, B)
calls the C function
.Fn F
with the values of
.Va A
and
.Va B .
A variable declared
.Sy COMMON
is a global symbol.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl S
Stop after writing the assembly of the machine,
.Pa NAME.s .
.It Fl Sir
Stop after writing the LLVM IR as text,
.Pa NAME.ll .
.It Fl H Ar file
A file of declarations, read in front of each source and part of it.
.It Fl c
Stop after generating the object file,
.Pa NAME.o .
.El
.Pp
There is no optimization flag: the environment variable
.Ev IMPOPT
(
.Ql -O0
to
.Ql -O3 ,
.Ql -Os ,
.Ql -Oz )
sets the level, and
.Fl Os
is the default, as for
.Xr imp 1 .
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl o Ar output
Name of the output file.
The default is the first source's name without
.Pa .imp ,
in the current directory.
.It Fl s Ar letters
Switches for the compiler proper, one letter each, as in the original:
listings and dumps of its tables.
They go to the standard error.
.Ql A
the code chain,
.Ql T
the code tree,
.Ql S
the semantics array,
.Ql X
the syntax graph,
.Ql I
the directory of names.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev IMPSYNTAX , Ev IMPFFI , Ev IMPLIB
Other files for the syntax, the C interface and the runtime library.
.It Ev TMPDIR
The directory for the private directory that holds the object of a program to be linked,
when it is an absolute path naming a directory in which one can be made.
The default is
.Pa tmp/
of the source tree, and
.Pa /tmp
for an installed compiler.
No temporary file is made for
.Fl S ,
.Fl Sir
or
.Fl c ,
and none for a link on Linux while an unnamed memory file can be used.
The directory and its files are deleted on exit and on
.Dv SIGHUP ,
.Dv SIGINT ,
.Dv SIGQUIT
and
.Dv SIGTERM .
.It Ev IMPNOMEMFD
If set and not
.Ql 0 ,
a link on Linux uses the private directory and not an unnamed memory file.
.It Ev IMPLDSHOW
If set, the arguments of the linker are printed.
.It Ev SDKROOT
On macOS, the SDK to link with.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /usr/share/imp/syntax.imc
The syntax of IMP72.
.It Pa /usr/share/imp/ffi.imc
The extension that defines
.Sy CCALL ,
.Sy EXTERNAL ,
.Sy CALLBACK ,
.Sy FNPTR
and
.Sy STRUCT .
.It Pa /usr/lib/imp/libimp.a
The runtime library.
.El
.Pp
The seed finds these relative to its own place.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr imp 1
.Sh AUTHORS
The original IMP72 is by
.An Walter Bilofsky ;
the IMP language by
.An Ned Irons .
